field for obvious reasons  and a curious thing happened as soon as  the national guard was withdrawn the  strikers disappeared  no more shots were fired they faded back  into the hillsides into the camps into  the ten colonies that were left  their enemies were the national guard  and once a national guard was withdrawn  from the district they figured their war  was over  the strike went on a few more months  officially uh but by then  you know all the blood had been spent  and the union lost the organization  battle on the end  it wasn't it was in september 19  december 1914 when they finally declared  the strike officially over but by then  the coal mines were back working at full  capacity and the unions were essentially  routed  so as i tell people since i started  doing this in essence the  the miners themselves rose up against  this oppressive system and they won the  shooting war but they lost the labor war  they lost the contract battle  that's it  love to  thank you  [Applause]  love to entertain questions if you have  any uh if you do have one raise your  hand and i will very slowly point at you  so they can move the microphone over and  and get your question  and if there's anything about the  coalfield war that you know about are  curious about i didn't read about  because you know i left an awful lot out  i'm more than happy to answer questions  yes in the back i'm pointing slowly  scott um in your book you you do a great  job of setting the stage describing the  life in the mining camps  um  what what were the key demands  for this strike  what were the miners seeking as far as  improvement in their lives  eight hour work day  the right to shop outside the company  stores  uh the right to  have a state i'm sorry an independent  check wayman to weigh the coal they  brought out so they wouldn't be ripped  off by the coal operators but as i think  i mentioned earlier the key issue the  one that mattered the most to them was  the right to organize you know they  wanted the union representation  and you could argue that and as the  companies did that that was actually  something that the union was fighting  for you know not the strikers um but at  that point you know when things got ugly  when the violence happened it was more  than union officials you know who were  firing those guns you know it was the  rank and file and it's clear as you read  through the book and read through you  know all the research and there's a ton  of great research here in denver  available you know first first level  research stuff  um that these guys these guys were  fighting for dignity and they were they  were fighting  uh to  overthrow that that sort of yoke of of  oppressive politics and oppressive  economics  a question over here  yes scott uh  number one uh i've got a couple the  first is did you run across phil van  sice at all in your  in your research on this and then number  two do you really consider this a  massacre  uh van ciser ran across he had some  great stuff and it's in the book so if  you've got a copy you can you can read  about it  he was a very significant figure in sort  of the internal uh national guard  you know obviously you're familiar with  him but but he was a big figure in how  they were addressing what they had done  uh and no i don't think it was a  massacre um  it was tragic obviously  uh anytime 11 kids and two mothers die  of suffocation and conditions like that  it's deplorable but your concept of a  massacre usually involves intentional  directed violence you envision machine  guns shotguns machetes you know it's  kind of an act a forceful act to kill  people  and i'm not a lawyer but you know  my gut feeling is this was at worst  criminally negligent homicide there's no  way to  conclude that the national guard knew  these people were hiding in this hole  under the ground which by the way was  dug initially as a maternity ward they  had no place for pregnant women to give  birth so they dug this big underground  cavern and  you know it's sort of the maternity  chamber  there's no way they could know they were  in there and they torched the tents and  they died as a result of the arson so to  me is criminal homicide a tragedy but  yeah  and what's kind of interesting is a  journalist understand this completely  it's a very dramatic story but to me the  cold-blooding blooded killing by the  national guardsmen of these three guys  that they had captured who are  defenseless is the bigger atrocity yeah  that was an intentional act by these  guys and that was quickly trumped  uh by the attention paid to the women  and children  it was a great story and the unions did  a very solid job of spinning that story  you know they the two women crawled out  of that pit two survived and they put  them on the road and they  did fundraising speeches all across the  country they address congress i think  one of them had a white house visit you  know they did a very good job this is  sort of you know one of the first  examples of sort of modern spin  campaigns  the companies the corporate side is  doing that as well with their own  people uh question in the back corner  yes sir in your research  were the rockefellers and the cfi  really fearful of a cut in production  and therefore productivity profits or  did they look upon the miners  as sub-humans inferior beings  and they could do anything they want  it this is a that's a great question and  the answer is full of all sorts of  nuance um  rockefeller seniors had a kind of an  operating policy of hiring what he  thought were good people  and letting them do their thing so it  was kind of a hands-off  approach to management  and as we know now that can lead to some  pretty disastrous  circumstances  i don't think they were driven by  prophets that much i've read a lot of  the telegrams obviously they're driven  by prophets but in their actions in  colorado i don't think they were they're  driven more by ideology the rockefellers  were stridently anti-union and there are  some quotes in here  from speeches by rockefeller jr who was  kind of running the show at that point  now that he'd willingly risk his entire  fortune to keep unions out i mean it was  a philosophical point with these guys  and lamont montgomery bowers who was  their man here in colorado i was cut  from the same cloth you know  they saw themselves as paternalistic  looking out for their own people  but uh bowers especially was uh you know  as you mentioned you know their approach  to their workers he thought they're a  bunch of just scum just riff-raff and  there's a wonderful telegram  unfortunately i took my bookmark out  there's a wonderful telegram from him  to rockefeller junior uh talking about  how he wouldn't mind  uh  they're talking about a cut in wages  well before the strike and he says he  wouldn't mind doing that to these miners  because they weren't really americans  anyway they're just here to earn money  build up a bank account back in their  home country  drink their i think said  eat their bread and drink their cheap  beer and then go back to their own  countries and he had no qualms about  cutting the wages for people like that  because they weren't real americans you  know so there's a lot of xenophobia  involved in it a lot of classism a lot  of racism  yes  one other thing  you had indicated that the umw  actually lost  as far as their demands but  cf and i started a company union that  gave them everything that the umw had  been had been striking for yeah and that  was an attempt by the company to sort of  fend off any future organizing campaigns  um and that sort of fizzled in the 30s  with the wagner act which outlawed the  community but it took 20 years well  actually in 1927 the wobblies organized  the mines so  even with the company union it sort of  came back  when haywood came back i'm sorry i said  when big bill haywood came back right  right yeah that's that's another  fascinating story  because i know that one is a local  activist named richard myers who  recently authored co-authored a book on  you know that strike  it's a fascinating history in and of  itself but it's interesting there wasn't  united mine workers who won that one was  the wallabies who won that one  uh  wait for the boom right there we go okay  she found you  of the 75 people that died how many were  actually guardsmen or soldiers and how  many were minors or how does that break  down um i wish i had that written down  on the side i have it in the book it was  first of all the 75 figure is a floor  that's all the ones that i could confirm  and i'm pretty convinced that i probably  missed four or five in some of the  outlying counties where i couldn't get  to the to the records a limited amount  of time to do the research so 75 sort of  the minimum  for  this gets very cold sounding but i  subtract the  13 victims at ludlow from that death  list because there were accidental  deaths in essence or  unintentional deaths and of the  remaining i think 38 were mine guards uh  strike breakers yeah they were on the  company side and the rest were the  striking miners  so obviously the strikers lost far fewer  people than they were then they wound up  killing and even when you include the  women and children you know it was  almost a draw at that point  it's kind of a cold way to look at it  but that's what wars are about  any other questions  great thank you very much for the coming  appreciate the time  blood passion scott martell is a staff  writer for the los angeles times for  more on his work visit  dot  scottmartel.com
 

